WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:11.800
<v Announcer>A quick warning-- there are curse words that are unbeeped in today's episode of the show. If you prefer a beeped version, you can find that at our website, thisamericanlife.org.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:45.640
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>It's This American Life. I'm Aviva DeKornfeld, sitting in for Ira Glass. So there's this recording I heard a couple months ago. And when I heard it, I could not believe it existed. I'd never heard anything like it. It was recorded at a hotel bar at the Vancouver airport by this woman named Vienna Luke. She'd arranged to meet with this guy, Justin, who she hadn't seen in almost a decade. She's sitting at a table. She orders a tea, sipping it slowly while she waits for him to arrive. She's wired for sound.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:01:04.070
<v Vienna Luke>Every so often, I'm kind of glancing to where the hostess desk is. And then I see Justin walk in across the restaurant, and he's asking the hostess where I'm sitting. Or he's saying, I'm meeting someone here.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:10.090
<v Vienna Luke>Oh, I think the guy's here. Dang, he looks exactly the same.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:28.490
<v Vienna Luke>And I say, dang, he looks exactly the same. Like, he is exactly who he has always been. He has not changed. And he has no idea what's coming because he doesn't know I'm different.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:48.530
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Vienna's got a hidden recorder in her purse sitting on the table and another recorder hidden in her bra. There are three cops in plain clothes sitting at tables around her, and a bunch more upstairs in a hotel room. Justin spots Vienna, walks over to her table, and Vienna jumps right in, with a nervous preamble.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:02:16.090
<v Vienna Luke>OK, so, [CHUCKLES] yeah, obviously, I haven't really wanted to do this for quite some time. And-- but I actually have a notebook today, and I wrote notes because I want to make sure that I cover off everything that I want to. Whew, and we're going to take this at my pace. And--

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:19.370
<v Justin>Can I just ask before you start, are you recording this?

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.010
<v Vienna Luke>No.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:30.650
<v Justin>OK. It wouldn't matter to me because I feel this is your information, and you do with it whatever you want.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:03:02.700
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Vienna is secretly recording because she's pretty sure that years ago, Justin assaulted her. But she doesn't know for sure. She has big gaps in her memory from that night. Memory gaps are pretty common in cases of sexual assault, which makes for a horrible situation because the one person who could clear things up and tell you what happened, the person who assaulted you, is the least likely person to talk to you and tell you the truth. Because why would they?

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:57.900
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>In Vienna's case, though, the man, Justin, actually agreed to sit down with her and talk about everything, to answer all of her questions in detail. I think most of us walk around with blank spots in our lives, small ones, big ones. And a lot of the time, you just give up on trying to get answers, accepting that you're just going to wonder forever. So what's it like to actually get all the answers you spent so long wondering about? And where does that leave you? Most of our show today is going to be the story of this recording. It's really remarkable. Stay with us.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:16.020
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>It's This American Life. Act One, Known Unknowns. So Vienna is at this hotel bar at the Vancouver airport, wearing that hidden recorder. And just a heads-up, before we start, this story includes a bunch of explicit details and references to sexual assault.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:45.620
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The man in the recording, which we'll get to in a minute, his name is Justin. He's a father of four boys, a veterinarian, though he has a sort of finance guy look about him-- close-cropped hair, symmetrical face, wouldn't look out of place in a Patagonia vest. Vienna's a fitness instructor and mom to two kids. She's warm and sensitive and loves an inspirational quote. She and Justin are actually related. Justin is Vienna's brother-in-law, her oldest sister, Sunny's, husband.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:05:11.070
<v Vienna Luke>I met him when I was 10. And yeah, he felt like an older brother. And I'd only had sisters, so having a brother was exciting. And I thought he was charming. I mean, he was very self-assured. And I guess I thought that was cool.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:29.510
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>For 14 years, Vienna has wanted to ask Justin about one night from when she was 20 and he was 31. She was living with Sunny and Justin, up in their small northern Canadian town, where they'd moved after they got married. And one day, when Sunny was away, Justin asked Vienna--

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:38.270
<v Vienna Luke>Well, do you want to get drunk to experience what it's like to be drunk in a safe place? That was the premise.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:06:03.950
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Which sounded reasonable to Vienna. She and her family were evangelical Christians. She went to church every Sunday. Justin was part of the leadership. And she'd had friends in the church who'd gotten drunk for the first time under a similar arrangement. So Justin makes Vienna a few mixed drinks to try-- a strawberry daiquiri, a piña colada, some kind of Kahlua thing, exactly the kind of drinks you might order for yourself if you were 20 and didn't like the taste of alcohol.

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:44.310
<v Vienna Luke>He's got music playing, like country music. And after I'd had a couple sips, he was dancing with me, two-stepping with me. And I feel like, already then, I was starting to feel a little bit woozy. And then I basically lose feeling in my legs and collapse on the ottoman. And I'm like, oh, my goodness, if this is what it feels like to be drunk, this is rough. Why do people do this?

00:06:44.310 --> 00:07:02.040
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Vienna passes out. A little while later, she comes to in her bedroom, which is in the basement. She has no clue how she got there from the living room upstairs. And she only has one more brief memory from the evening. Warning for the listeners-- this next detail might be upsetting.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:29.720
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The one moment Vienna woke up, she remembers having an out-of-body experience, looking down at herself, seeing Justin taking off her underwear. The next morning, Vienna wakes up with a stomach ache and a bad feeling. She tells Justin that she's not sure exactly what happened, but that she thinks he should tell Sunny. He brushes her off, saying there's no need to tell her. It'll just trouble her. And besides, nothing happened anyway.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:35.020
<v Vienna Luke>I'm like, OK, yeah, that's what I've been trying to tell myself, too, as this memory comes up.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.320
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>You're almost hoping for that answer--

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:37.820
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah, no.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.500
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>--to be like, OK, it's nothing, right? It's nothing.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:53.360
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah, it's nothing. I mean, even in the moment, I remember telling myself, he's taking off my underwear because I must have vomited. I must have gotten--

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.980
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>This must be a brotherly, familial act.

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:13.320
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah, like, oh, he's just changing my clothes because I threw up on myself. So I'd say things like that to myself. And then I'd be like, but why would he have to change my underwear?

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:53.170
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>So this night happens. And Vienna has to decide, does she tell her family? And if she does, what exactly is she even telling them if she doesn't know any of the details? Vienna's family is one that makes a point to spend a lot of time all together-- Vienna's parents, her two sisters, and her. Vienna's the baby. She grew up particularly close with her oldest sister, Sunny, Justin's wife. Sunny is 10 years older than Vienna, so she was more like a second mom to her. Sunny would dote on her, read Junie B. Jones to her at night. She painted a mural of farm animals on her bedroom wall. When Vienna got older, they'd bake chocolate chip cookies together.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:09:17.930
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>But for months after that night, Vienna doesn't tell anyone about what happened, until at the end of a family vacation, Vienna's middle sister, Shawnessy, notices Justin pull Vienna into this long hug, a hug that looked too intimate to her eye. So Shawnessy, being the loud, confrontational one in the family, corners Vienna and badgers the truth out of her.

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:53.450
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Vienna stumbles through a partial explanation, saying Justin gave her alcohol, but she can't bring herself to mention the one thing she remembers about the night, about him taking off her underwear. But even without that detail, something about the way Vienna was talking, her body language, Shawnessy thought, Justin must have done something inappropriate. So she convinces Vienna to tell their parents. And Vienna tells them what she told Shawnessy. Vienna's parents are very surprised she got drunk. Vienna's dad calls Justin to get answers about what exactly happened.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:10:07.170
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Justin, of course, downplays everything, saying Vienna just drank too much, that's all. When Justin's wife, Sunny, hears about all this, she shuts down entirely. Sunny's not her real name, by the way.

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:38.570
<v Vienna Luke>My sister didn't want to know anything and basically pretended like it didn't happen. I think everyone was just so confused. They didn't know what to do with it. And at the end of the day, people just-- they just want the family to be together. So pretty much, it was just like life as usual after that. And I would have had to see him at the next family thing.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:11:05.660
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>There's a terrible ordinariness to sexual violence. I don't know any woman who hasn't, at some point, wondered if a man was going to hurt her. What's extraordinary about Vienna's story, though, is how she chose to respond to what happened to her. But that doesn't happen for years, because for almost a decade, Vienna tried to continue on like normal. She's naturally a peacemaker, and pushing the issue with Justin just seemed like it would cause problems for everyone.

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:39.660
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Plus, at this point, Vienna is depressed, operating on autopilot, and didn't have the wherewithal to force her family to confront a thing she wasn't ready to deal with herself. So instead, she went to university, spent time with her family, tried to figure out what she wanted to do with her life, but she couldn't shake the unsettled feeling she'd had ever since the night Justin gave her alcohol. And eventually, the uncertainty about what happened to her that night became this kind of torture, just question marks looping nonstop in the back of her brain as she went about daily life.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:12:03.980
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Then, in 2014, seven years after the night Justin assaulted her, Vienna's oldest sister Sunny discovered that Justin was having an affair-- multiple affairs, actually. This news rocked their community. Everyone had seen him as this wholesome family man, an elder in the church. Sunny ended up taking Justin back after a couple months. But for Vienna--

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:16.900
<v Vienna Luke>It was this affirmation that he was capable of not being a nice guy. I finally believed myself.

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:19.980
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>And what was it exactly that you believed?

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:25.660
<v Vienna Luke>That he had done something to me, that something had happened.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:56.630
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>So Vienna cut Justin out. Shawnessy did as well. She'd always believed Justin had done something to Vienna, even though at this point, Vienna still hadn't told her the detail about the underwear. Both refused to be in the same room as Justin. Vienna's parents positioned themselves as a kind of see-no-evil Switzerland in the middle of this newfound estrangement, with Vienna and Shawnessy on one side and Justin and Sunny on the other. They felt uneasy about what they knew, but they didn't know how else to handle it.

00:12:56.630 --> 00:13:27.630
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>And so the entire family structure was reorganized to accommodate this vague, undiscussed thing that happened. For a family that loves spending time together, this meant no more holidays, no more family vacations. They all settled into a painful, awkward, strange, new normal for a few years, until one night, Vienna called her middle sister, Shawnessy, and said, I'm finally ready to figure out what happened to me. It had been 12 years since the night.

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:59.790
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>In order to go to the police, Vienna and Shawnessy figure they need more evidence than Vienna's one memory of the night. So together, they make a list of everyone they knew Justin had been close with at the time of the assault, but wasn't close to anymore-- old coworkers, former friends. They figured, maybe he had talked to someone about what he'd done. Vienna didn't want to talk to any of these people herself. So Shawnessy calls roughly a dozen people, including one of Justin's former employees at the vet clinic he ran.

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:32.830
<v Vienna Luke>And she says, I know why you're calling. He drugged your sister. And continues to tell my sister everything-- like, what she knows, that they were working in the back one day in the clinic, and he says, if you were giving whatever drug it was to a human, how much would you give them to make sure they were out?

00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:52.640
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Another former employee told Shawnessy that one day, the receptionist at the clinic was looking for something on the work computer and accidentally came across pictures of Justin with a naked woman who might have been Vienna. The woman's face wasn't visible. Some of the pictures were selfies Justin took, leaning against the woman's body.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:15:29.360
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>With those two pieces of information about the drugs and the pictures, Vienna and Shawnessy go to the police. Most sexual assault allegations that get reported, they never go anywhere. But Vienna gets extraordinarily lucky by getting the ear of a cop named Joelle LaChance, who's made tricky sexual assault cases her life's work. She's like one of those cops on a TV show whose secret sauce comes down to "she just really gives a shit." She used her vacation days to fly places for this case. She was late to her own sister's funeral because she was helping set up Vienna's Vancouver recording.

00:15:29.360 --> 00:16:02.760
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The idea of going to the police more than a decade after an assault with only one memory of what happened and hearsay from a couple former acquaintances and being taken seriously seems unimaginable. But that is what happened. Joelle ropes the undercover police team in to work with Vienna on her case, and they come up with a plan. For years, since Vienna first distanced herself from Justin, he had been emailing her periodically, asking her to return to the family. Vienna never responded to those emails.

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:39.730
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>But now, with the help of the police, she crafted a response, saying, basically, I'm willing to move past this, but in order for me to do that, I need you to tell me what happened that night. Justin writes back and says very clearly that he sexually assaulted her. Vienna thinks, OK, that was easy. We got him. But the police say they need more concrete evidence, something undeniable. A lawyer in court could argue that someone other than Justin had written that email to Vienna. And they needed specifics about what exactly he did.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:17:05.770
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>So Vienna writes Justin again, saying, we need to meet in person, and you need to tell me the details of what happened. That's the only way I'll be able to move forward. Justin, miraculously, agrees. Vienna thinks it's because he figured he would get to be the hero if he could be the one to reunite the family. Justin offers to fly to Vancouver, where Vienna lives. So they decide to meet at the airport.

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:30.330
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The cops picked the airport because even though they figured Justin wasn't likely an active threat to Vienna, they knew that if he had just walked off an airplane, there was no way he could have a weapon on him. And Vienna liked the airport idea, too. It felt comfortingly anonymous. In preparation for the meeting, the lead undercover cop spent a couple of days training Vienna on how to get usable information out of Justin.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:42.070
<v Vienna Luke>So the day I did training, I brought in my notebook, and he was like, whoa, this is so weird. You have a notebook. Nobody brings a notebook, because usually--

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:42.770
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Wait, why not?

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:58.190
<v Vienna Luke>Because, usually, he's working with-- he's like, you are very different than the undercover people I usually work with, which, not to stereotype, but many of them are criminals that he's--

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:01.450
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Like he's flipped them, and then they're going to work against--

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:01.950
<v Vienna Luke>Yes, yeah.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:02.350
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>--their former--

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:12.170
<v Vienna Luke>So a bunch of the stuff we were going through in this contract, he's like, this isn't going to apply to you, but you can't commit any other crimes throughout this operation. He's like that.

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:13.890
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>And you're like, no problem.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:15.090
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah. [CHUCKLES]

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:34.140
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>So Vienna, A+ student with her little notebook, writes down everything she needs to get from Justin. And then she and the undercover cop role-play so she can practice pressing Justin when he tries to give her evasive non-answers. And a few weeks later, Vienna heads to the Vancouver airport.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:53.420
<v Vienna Luke>I feel like I came here today, or know I came here today, wanting you to give me details on what happened. So I hope that you're ready to tell me the actual facts today.

00:18:53.420 --> 00:19:22.380
<v Justin>Well, the things that I learned is that when there's disclosure, it's traumatic. And the best way is to have it all happen at once. So I don't want there to be any amount of information that has to be disclosed at a different point in time. This is the time. So you will get everything you need from me today. Because it will be more traumatic for you if you had to do it again.

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:51.820
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Justin uses this bizarre therapy speak all throughout their conversation. Several years before this meeting, Justin went to counseling for sex addiction, and it seems like that's where he picked this up, this language that allows him to simultaneously acknowledge that something happened to Vienna, while also distancing himself from it. It's like he's saying, I can help you heal from this, while totally ignoring the fact that he's the reason she needs to heal in the first place.

00:19:51.820 --> 00:20:24.630
<v Justin>So throughout that night-- and I don't mean to go into other things. This whole objectification of women in our culture right now is what had happened for me in that time. And for some reason, I had a line drawn. I wasn't going to touch you in a sexual way.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:27.690
<v Vienna Luke>OK, but you're not actually telling me what happened.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:40.030
<v Justin>No, I know. Sorry. And it's just sort of the rules before I start. So the rule for myself, everything that happened, was on this side of that line for me.

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:41.530
<v Vienna Luke>The rules before you started what?

00:20:41.530 --> 00:20:48.050
<v Justin>Before that night started. And again, I wouldn't say that they were even pretty thought-out rules and stuff.

00:20:48.050 --> 00:20:53.210
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah, OK, let's get back to the night and what actually-- tell me what happened.

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:54.030
<v Justin>OK.

00:20:54.030 --> 00:21:05.910
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>You're so impressively uncompromising in the tape. And every time he tries to stray and talk about the broader issue of objectification of women, you force him right back to the topic at hand.

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:33.550
<v Vienna Luke>I feel like I went into it with a mentality of, like, I cannot fail. I can't-- this is my one chance. I can't mess this up. It wasn't even an option. I remember my coach, my undercover cop, saying, he's going to try to make light of this. He's going to try to pull you off track. And you just have to keep bringing the focus back.

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:34.770
<v Vienna Luke>Tell me what happened.

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:40.710
<v Justin>OK. I encouraged you to drink alcohol.

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:41.470
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:42.530
<v Justin>You had never been drunk.

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:43.950
<v Vienna Luke>Were you drinking with me?

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:45.990
<v Justin>Yes.

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:48.370
<v Vienna Luke>I don't remember that.

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:59.420
<v Justin>I didn't drink very much because I didn't want to be drunk.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:02.910
<v Vienna Luke>Mm-hmm.

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:10.870
<v Justin>I had done that in advance, setting this up as an opportunity for you to see what it was like to be drunk.

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:12.730
<v Vienna Luke>Well, yes, that's what you told me.

00:22:12.730 --> 00:22:13.230
<v Justin>Right.

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:14.770
<v Vienna Luke>But that's not what happened.

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:15.270
<v Justin>No.

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:17.350
<v Vienna Luke>[LAUGHS]

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:24.440
<v Justin>I remember that you laid down on the ottoman on your back.

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:49.360
<v Vienna Luke>Oh, I did not lay down. I lost feeling in my legs. And that's not what happens when someone gets drunk. [LAUGHS] So are you going to tell me what you did, or am I-- yeah, I didn't just lose feeling in my legs for nothing. And I've been drunk since.

00:22:49.360 --> 00:23:09.600
<v Justin>Right. I put something in one of the drinks. I was nervous about it, and I didn't know how much. And so I was cautious. And it was-- I can't imagine that it did anything to you. But maybe the combination is what did it. But I did put something in one of the drinks.

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:11.120
<v Vienna Luke>And what was it?

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:14.680
<v Justin>I'm not sure if it was Valium or if it was Ketamine.

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:19.440
<v Vienna Luke>You're not sure? Well, that doesn't make me feel like--

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:21.320
<v Justin>I know that I had both there.

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:23.640
<v Vienna Luke>Do you know how much you gave me?

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:37.940
<v Justin>No, I don't. It wasn't something that I'd done before, so I didn't know how much was appropriate. But I certainly didn't want to hurt anybody. I didn't want to hurt you. I remember you on your back on the ottoman. And you started--

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:38.640
<v Waiter>You guys OK with everything?

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:40.000
<v Justin>We're good. Thank you.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:53.240
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Justin's talking to a waiter here, who's asking if they have everything they need. I don't have anything smart to say about this. I just think it's insane to have to interact with a waiter while in the middle of a conversation like this.

00:23:53.240 --> 00:24:31.090
<v Justin>You were throwing up, so I helped you down the stairs to your bedroom. There was vomit on your clothing. And so I helped you out of your clothes. That's an odd way to phrase that. And the pictures that I took during that time were of you on the bed.

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:47.330
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Vienna now presses Justin for specifics about the pictures, just as the undercover cop instructed. She needs to get Justin to corroborate the fact that he gave her drugs, took off her clothes, and took pictures of her, with as many details as possible.

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:50.810
<v Vienna Luke>How many photos did you take?

00:24:50.810 --> 00:24:52.930
<v Justin>30.

00:24:52.930 --> 00:24:55.610
<v Vienna Luke>Was I wearing anything in any of them?

00:24:55.610 --> 00:24:56.970
<v Justin>No.

00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:59.170
<v Vienna Luke>Can you describe them for me?

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:30.970
<v Justin>There's a picture of you on your stomach from above. There's a picture of you on your back, showing your chest. There's a full-body picture of you from above with you laying on your back. I took a picture of us, like a selfie, at the time, of you on your back with me, with my head down close to your chest.

00:25:30.970 --> 00:25:57.530
<v Justin>I took a picture of you on your side with my penis close to your face. But I never touched you with it. But there, again, none of the pictures-- I don't know if these details are important to you. Your legs are never open for any of those shots. I don't have any pictures of your genitalia.

00:25:57.530 --> 00:26:01.850
<v Vienna Luke>So the pictures were from the belly button up?

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:06.300
<v Justin>No, from the waist down as well, but only from the front and closed leg.

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:09.790
<v Vienna Luke>It's only from the front because you had a line drawn.

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:11.620
<v Justin>I wasn't going to position you--

00:26:11.620 --> 00:26:12.840
<v Vienna Luke>With my legs open.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:26.540
<v Justin>Right. I had those pictures for a while afterwards. I was always disappointed that I didn't have a picture of the vagina. But I was never willing to position you to get one.

00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:56.180
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>If we take Justin at his word, it tells us something interesting, which is how he lives with what he's done. He's come up with a story he can tell himself so that he's still a good person because there are lines he drew and rules he set that he stayed on the right side of. I suspect that's why he agreed to talk to Vienna at all, and why there isn't a shred of remorse in his voice, because he doesn't really think of himself as a guy who did anything worth apologizing for.

00:26:56.180 --> 00:27:20.060
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Of course, just to say, for the rest of us who live in the real world, drugging your sister-in-law, stripping off all her clothes, and taking naked pictures of her for later use, none of that is anywhere close to being on the right side of any line.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:31.540
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>So Justin has his justifications. But then there's some stuff that he has a really hard time explaining, try as he might. Listen to how he dodges this very simple question.

00:27:31.540 --> 00:27:37.780
<v Vienna Luke>What was your intent? Why did you take the pictures?

00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:44.380
<v Justin>Sorry. I had a tough time talking about this and staying on top of going into the greater issue with pornography.

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:50.980
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah, but it's about you, and it's about me. So stick to it as much as you can.

00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:52.300
<v Justin>Right.

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:56.500
<v Vienna Luke>Because it was you that looked at them, not the greater world that has--

00:27:56.500 --> 00:27:57.000
<v Justin>No, I know.

00:27:56.860 --> 00:27:57.360
<v Vienna Luke>--problems with pornography.

00:27:57.260 --> 00:28:07.160
<v Justin>It's just the issue of pornography, just having pornography, right? Like, if you go around to computers, go door to door and start pulling computers apart, you'll find pornography on most of them.

00:28:07.160 --> 00:28:11.410
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah, hopefully not a person's sister-in-law, but you know.

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:12.170
<v Justin>Right.

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:38.150
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>This is what got me in the recording, the way Vienna is like a prosecutor, quick and sharp and relentless, except she's cross-examining her own perpetrator. Like so many survivors, it took Vienna a long time to classify what happened to her as sexual assault. And that's in part because of who the perpetrator was-- her cool, charming, churchgoing brother-in-law, family.

00:28:38.150 --> 00:29:08.910
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>But one of the reasons she's putting herself through this meeting with Justin is also family. The way she sees it, recording this conversation is her best chance at getting through to her sister, Sunny. Vienna hates how distant she and Sunny have become, how estranged. Sunny and her kids will visit Vienna and Shawnessy once or twice a year, but there's none of the intimacy they used to have, because Vienna's never gotten to have an open, honest conversation with Sunny about what happened to her.

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:43.390
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>The one time they came close was back when Sunny discovered Justin's affairs. She finally called Vienna and asked her about the night Justin got her drunk. And Vienna tried to tell her, but she said, pretty quickly, she realized Sunny didn't really want to understand. She was more there on behalf of Justin, as a kind of spokesperson. She kept defending him, saying things like, you don't understand. He was exposed to pornography when he was a kid. He had a sex addiction. He's a victim himself. It went nowhere.

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:53.390
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>But with this recording, Sunny would finally hear the truth about what happened. And then she might leave Justin once and for all. That was Vienna's hope.

00:29:53.390 --> 00:29:55.390
<v Vienna Luke>Who saw them? I mean--

00:29:55.390 --> 00:30:13.600
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>So Vienna is now about an hour into her hour and a half long conversation with Justin. And you can hear, the way Justin's speaking to her, that he still playing this knowing authority. And Vienna is sitting across from him like, I'm not who you think I am. I don't defer to you.

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:19.800
<v Vienna Luke>I kind of don't believe that you just took pictures of me naked.

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:55.520
<v Justin>So, I guess, the reason that I wanted to draw that distinction between sexual contact and non-sexual contact is because there was contact that I felt was-- I remember I felt really close to you at that time. So I laid down beside you, and I put my arm around you, but I was careful that I didn't touch your breasts when I put my arm around you because that--

00:30:55.520 --> 00:30:56.020
