00:38:43.520 --> 00:38:47.260
<v Vienna Luke>Well, I lived with you at two separate locations.

00:38:47.260 --> 00:38:47.760
<v Justin>Both of them.

00:38:47.560 --> 00:38:54.480
<v Vienna Luke>So both of them. So how many pictures of me have you or did you have?

00:38:54.480 --> 00:38:58.600
<v Justin>8, 10 pictures. They were blurry. They weren't good quality.

00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:01.680
<v Vienna Luke>How many occasions did you do this on?

00:39:01.680 --> 00:39:03.720
<v Justin>Three or four.

00:39:03.720 --> 00:39:16.480
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Justin just admitted to a whole other crime that Vienna and the cops hadn't even intended to get out of him. And he does it in his detached, neutral, unapologetic way.

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:21.700
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>It's glaring that in the whole hour and a half long conversation you guys had, he never says sorry.

00:39:21.700 --> 00:39:26.760
<v Vienna Luke>No, absolutely. There's definitely, like, yeah, no remorse.

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:30.280
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Would it have meant anything to you for him to apologize?

00:39:30.280 --> 00:39:40.840
<v Vienna Luke>I mean, if he was capable of apologizing and if it actually meant something, then, yeah, I would have taken it. But I don't believe he's capable.

00:39:40.840 --> 00:39:56.610
<v Vienna Luke>I guess, thank you for coming down here and for meeting with me and for, I feel like-- well, for finally being honest. Because let's face it, you've had 14 years.

00:39:56.610 --> 00:40:14.850
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Vienna spends an excruciating few minutes trying to flag down a waiter to pay for her tea. Before he leaves, Justin hands Vienna a goodie bag of cookies that Sunny had baked for her, along with a bunch of carrots from their garden. They say goodbye, and Vienna runs to the bathroom.

00:40:14.850 --> 00:40:22.890
<v Vienna Luke>And I think that's where it all hit my body. And it's almost like I'd been holding my breath the whole time.

00:40:22.890 --> 00:40:27.890
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Yeah. What's it like when it's hitting your body? What does it feel like?

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:34.410
<v Vienna Luke>[EXHALES] The stress of all the detail was almost being downloaded to my body.

00:40:34.410 --> 00:40:46.490
<v Vienna Luke>Oh, dear. Oh, dear. [CRYING] Oh, dear. Oh, no.

00:40:46.490 --> 00:40:55.450
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Vienna waits a minute to make sure Justin's really gone, and then heads upstairs to the hotel room to meet up with her sister, Shawnessy, and the undercover team.

00:40:55.450 --> 00:41:11.010
<v Vienna Luke>I knock on the door. Somebody opens it. And they are all practically at the door. They're as close as the door as they physically all could be. And it was so visible on everyone--

00:41:11.010 --> 00:41:13.650
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>What does their face look like? Yeah.

00:41:13.650 --> 00:41:22.210
<v Vienna Luke>[SIGHS] It's like celebration, like shock and awe, and whoa, you did it.

00:41:22.210 --> 00:41:47.740
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Vienna pulled it off. The meeting really couldn't have been more of a success. This wire recording ended up being the central piece of evidence used in court. Justin is charged with one count of sexual assault, two counts of voyeurism, and one count of administering a stupefying drug. He pleads guilty to all but the drug charge. Vienna assumes he did this to try to avoid losing his vet license.

00:41:47.740 --> 00:42:10.900
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Justin ended up getting a sentence of two years minus a day of house arrest, where he was still allowed to leave the house for work and church and errands. The crown counsel appealed. The Court of Appeals eventually overruled the original sentence, and Justin had to serve out his term in federal prison, though, after just six months in prison, Justin was granted parole.

00:42:10.900 --> 00:42:35.980
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Whatever you think of his sentence, this result is exceptionally rare. The vast majority of sexual assaults are, of course, never reported to the police. And in Canada, of the cases that are reported and make it to court, only about 6% result in jail time for the perpetrator. So those are the sort of satisfying legal consequences.

00:42:35.980 --> 00:42:56.780
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>But remember, Vienna went into this because ever since Justin assaulted her, she'd had this unsettled feeling, this itch to know what happened to her. And now that she knows, Vienna says that for the first time since that night, she's able to not think about what happened to her. She used to think about it all the time.

00:42:56.780 --> 00:43:23.220
<v Vienna Luke>It's kind of crazy how captive I was to everything that was done to me, the not knowing and how much it-- yeah, it dictated so much of my life. And now, I don't have to worry about it anymore. And I'm tired. And I'm just going to let the rest be.

00:43:23.220 --> 00:43:53.750
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>And then there's the last thing Vienna was hoping to get out of all of this. In some ways, it was the thing she wanted most. And that's getting her sister Sunny back. After the trial, once Vienna's parents learned the full extent of what Justin did to her, they cut Justin out. But Sunny is still married to him. Vienna had thought that if she could get Justin to lay out exactly what he did to her with all the graphic details, and if Sunny could hear that version of the story--

00:43:53.750 --> 00:44:08.510
<v Vienna Luke>My hope was that she would be able to [SIGHS] empathize, be able to be my sister, instead of being his wife. I thought if she knew the truth, it would change everything.

00:44:08.510 --> 00:44:29.590
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>But as far as Vienna knows, Sunny never heard the tape. She served as a witness for Justin in the trial, and so was kept out of the courtroom until it was her time to be called to the stand, which meant she missed all the wire tape that Vienna's lawyers played in court. I reached out to Sunny and Justin multiple times. I never heard back.

00:44:29.590 --> 00:44:44.470
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>It must be frustrating to do such a good job at getting the facts of what happened that night, and then for the facts to do nothing to persuade the person you were trying to convince the most that something did happen.

00:44:44.470 --> 00:45:08.290
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah. Yeah, it's really hard in terms of, look, I did this for you, and you don't want to see it. But also, I mean, I can't have a relationship if she can't recognize what happened or what was done to me. It's--

00:45:08.290 --> 00:45:10.410
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Yeah.

00:45:10.410 --> 00:45:20.990
<v Vienna Luke>I want a relationship with my sister. I love her deeply, but also, I'm not OK with her staying married to him. So I--

00:45:20.990 --> 00:45:27.670
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Yeah. They would need to break up in order for you to have a meaningfully close relationship with her again.

00:45:27.670 --> 00:45:29.230
<v Vienna Luke>Yes.

00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:33.630
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Do you hold out any hope of that happening?

00:45:33.630 --> 00:45:46.760
<v Vienna Luke>Yeah. As much as I need to move on and find peace, I always hold on to a little bit of hope.

00:45:46.760 --> 00:46:34.760
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>After Vienna's conversation with Justin, she drove home, exhaustion taking over as the adrenaline started to recede. She still had the goodie bag from Sunny. Vienna's partner, Jordan, thought she should get rid of it, throw the stuff away. But she didn't want to. She used to love baking cookies with Sunny. Maybe this was Sunny's oblique way of saying, I know everything is messed up. I know I chose Justin over you. I know things won't ever be the same. But I'm still here, and I still love you. That's what Vienna would like to believe, anyway. So she unwrapped the cookies and ate them.

00:46:34.760 --> 00:47:08.280
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>This story first came to us from the CBC. They made a whole four-part series about Vienna, called Personally-- Agent Layla. Their story goes far beyond the tape you heard today. There's a link to their series on our website, so go take a listen. If you've experienced sexual assault and want to talk to somebody, you can reach out to the National Sexual Assault Hotline, 1-800-656-HOPE.

00:47:08.280 --> 00:47:21.240
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Coming up, a person passes a test they had no idea they were taking. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.

00:47:21.240 --> 00:47:59.410
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>It's This American Life. I'm Aviva DeKornfeld, sitting in for Ira Glass. Today's show, The Missing Minutes. We've arrived at Act Two of our show, Act Two, Unknown Unknowns. So there's information you know you don't know. But then there's all the other stuff, stuff that just doesn't quite make sense, but you don't know why. You feel like you're missing something. So you hope that one day, it'll all be explained and finally made clear. This last story is a short piece of fiction about what that day might be like. It's by Miranda July. Here she is.

00:47:59.410 --> 00:48:24.810
<v Miranda July>Someone is getting excited. Somebody, somewhere, is shaking with excitement because something tremendous is about to happen to this person. This person has dressed for the occasion. This person's hoped and dreamed. And now it is really happening, and this person can hardly believe it. But believing is not an issue here. It is really, really happening.

00:48:24.810 --> 00:49:08.010
<v Miranda July>It involves stepping forward and bowing. Possibly, there is some kneeling, such as when one is knighted. One is almost never knighted. But this person may kneel and receive a tap on each shoulder with a sword. Or, more likely, this person will be in a car, or a store, or under a vinyl canopy when it happens, or online, or on the phone. It could be an email, Re-- Your Knighthood, or a long, laughing, rambling phone message in which every person this person has ever known is talking on a speakerphone, and they are all saying, you have passed the test. It was all just a test. We were only kidding. Real life is so much better than that.

00:49:08.010 --> 00:49:35.500
<v Miranda July>This person is laughing out loud with relief and playing the message back to get the address of the place where every person this person has ever known is waiting to hug this person and bring her into the fold of life. It is really exciting. And it's not just a dream. It's real.

00:49:35.500 --> 00:50:08.980
<v Miranda July>They are all waiting by a picnic table in a park this person has driven past many times before. There they are. It's everyone. There are balloons taped to the benches. And the girl this person used to stand next to at the bus stop is waving a streamer. Everyone is smiling. For a moment, this person is almost creeped out by the scene. But it would be so like this person to become depressed on the happiest day ever, so this person bucks up and joins the crowd.

00:50:08.980 --> 00:50:36.460
<v Miranda July>Teachers of subjects that this person wasn't even good at are kissing this person and renouncing the very subjects they taught. Math teachers are saying that math was just a funny way of saying, I love you. But now they are simply saying it-- I love you. And the chemistry and PE teachers are also saying it. And this person can tell they really mean it. It's totally amazing.

00:50:36.460 --> 00:51:15.180
<v Miranda July>Certain jerks and idiots and assholes appear from time to time, and it's as if they've had plastic surgery. Their faces are disfigured with love. The handsome assholes are plain and kind. And the ugly jerks are sweet, and they're folding this person's sweater and putting it somewhere where it won't get dirty. Best of all, every person this person has ever loved is there, even the ones who got away. They hold this person's hand and tell this person how hard it was to pretend to get mad and drive off and never come back.

00:51:15.180 --> 00:51:37.870
<v Miranda July>This person almost can't believe it. It seemed so real. This person's heart was broken and has healed, and now this person hardly knows what to think. This person is almost mad. But everyone soothes this person. Everyone explains that it was absolutely necessary to know how strong this person was.

00:51:37.870 --> 00:52:07.270
<v Miranda July>Oh, look, there's the doctor who prescribed the medicine that made this person temporarily blind, and the man who paid this person $2,000 to have sex with him three times when this person was very broke. Both these men are in attendance. They seem to know each other. They both have little medals that they're pinning on this person. They are badges of great honor and strength. The badges sparkle in the sunlight, and everyone cheers.

00:52:07.270 --> 00:52:39.070
<v Miranda July>This person suddenly feels the need to check her post office box. It is an old habit. And even if everything's going to be terrific from now on, this person still wants mail. This person says she'll be right back, and everyone this person has ever known says, fine, take your time. This person gets in her car and drives to the post office and opens the box, and there is nothing, even though it is Tuesday, which is a famously good day for mail.

00:52:39.070 --> 00:53:21.880
<v Miranda July>This person is so disappointed, this person gets back in the car and, having completely forgotten about the picnic, drives home and checks the voicemail, and there are no new messages, just the old one about passing the test and life being better. There are no emails either, probably because everyone is at the picnic. This person can't seem to go back to the picnic. This person realizes that staying home means blowing off everyone this person has ever known. But the desire to stay in is very strong. This person wants to run a bath and then read in bed.

00:53:21.880 --> 00:53:47.160
<v Miranda July>In the bathtub, this person pushes the bubbles around and listens to the sound of millions of them popping at once. This person pushes the bubbles onto their breasts and makes weird shapes with the foam. By now, everyone must have realized that this person is not coming back to the picnic. Everyone was wrong. This person is not who they thought this person was.

00:53:47.160 --> 00:54:17.320
<v Miranda July>This person plunges underwater. This person can stay underwater for an impressively long time, but only in a bathtub. This person wonders if there will ever be an Olympic contest for holding your breath under bathwater. If there were such a contest, this person would surely win it. An Olympic medal might redeem this person in the eyes of everyone this person has ever known. But no such contest exists, so there will be no redeeming.

00:54:17.320 --> 00:54:55.200
<v Miranda July>This person mourns the fact that she has ruined her one chance to be loved by everyone. As this person climbs into bed, the weight of this tragedy seems to bear down upon this person's chest. And it is a comforting weight, almost human in heft. This person sighs. This person's eyes begin to close. This person sleeps.

00:54:55.200 --> 00:55:13.600
<v Aviva DeKornfeld>Miranda July. She is the author of the book All Fours, which is the only book that I, upon completing, flipped over and immediately started reading again. I can't recommend it enough. Miranda also has a Substack called, appropriately, Miranda July. Check it out.

